# Experiment 14, the second answer: the same ore line, built in about 1880.
#
# The engine can go where you want it, which sounds like a small thing and is
# the whole difference. A water wheel has to be in the river; a steam engine is
# wherever the coal can be got to. So this plant is not laid out around a
# prime mover, it is laid out around a *boiler house*:
#
#   coal -> burner -> exchanger -> steam engine -> gearbox -> crushers
#                        ^                |
#                        |                v
#                     jacket <------- condensate
#                        ^                |
#                        +--- waste heat -+-> radiator
#
# Two things here are experiment 14 and nothing else.
#
# 1. The engine cannot be built without a cooling decision. It puts 500 heat a
#    tick into a cast-iron body that trips at 220 and sheds its own temperature
#    to the air, so left alone it climbs past its ceiling and trips. What keeps
#    it under 100 degrees is three separate decisions: a radiator on the waste
#    port, a jacket on the same port, and a clear tile of air around the engine
#    -- which costs plot and nothing else, and is the cheapest of the three.
#
# 2. The waste heat is not thrown away. It goes into the feedwater, which is the
#    second diagram in the note that asked for this experiment: the condensate
#    comes off the engine at band 1, passes through the jacket on its way back
#    to the boiler, and arrives two bands warmer carrying heat the engine was
#    trying to get rid of. Closing that loop is also why the valve on the makeup
#    line is a real setting with a cliff on both sides of it. An engine vents a
#    sixth of its steam up the stack, so the loop runs at a deficit; twelve a
#    tick covers most of it and the engine settles at 60% of rating. Open the
#    valve to fourteen and the plant does not get faster, it *floods*: the
#    surplus fills the feedwater line, which blocks the condensate, which stops
#    the engine, which stops the boiler, and the whole thing deadlocks with
#    every buffer full. That is the second era's other failure mode and it is
#    the one nobody expects. This plant draws twelve units of water a tick
#    where the first-era one draws four hundred.
#
# The drive train is one gearbox and three rigid wires, and nothing is isolated
# from anything, because steel rates 9, cast iron rates 7, and a crusher shakes
# at 7. There is not a `belt` anywhere in this file, and that is the whole of
# the second era: 19-waterline needs four of them and pays eight percent on
# every one, and this plant pays nothing because it can bolt its crushers
# straight to the drive.
#
# What it costs: 20 coal a tick, forever, and a machine that stops if the fire
# goes out.

machine "Steam Ore Line"
brief line

inlet     I1  at 0,0    draws ore
crusher   C1  at 4,0
crusher   C2  at 9,0
outlet    O1  at 14,0

gearbox   GB1 at 4,7   ratio 2

steamengine SE1 at 4,10
jacket    JK1 at 0,12
radiator  RD1 at 12,11

exchanger HX1 at 4,15
burner    BU1 at 9,15

valve     V1  at 1,19   limit 12
skip      SK1 at 9,11
pump      P1  at 4,19   draws water
inlet     F1  at 9,19   draws coal

wire F1.out -> BU1.fuel
wire BU1.heat -> HX1.heat
wire HX1.steam -> SE1.steam

wire SE1.exhaust -> SK1.vapour
wire SE1.waste -> JK1.heat
wire SE1.waste -> RD1.heat
wire SE1.condensate -> HX1.water
wire SE1.condensate -> JK1.water
wire JK1.out -> HX1.water

wire P1.water -> V1.in
wire V1.out -> HX1.water

wire SE1.rotary -> GB1.in
wire GB1.out -> C1.drive
wire GB1.out -> C2.drive

wire I1.out -> C1.in
wire C1.out -> C2.in
wire C2.out -> O1.solid
